  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n- Isolated Severe Storms Possible Today and Tonight, Large Hail and \n  Damaging Winds Possible. Timing and Coverage Is Uncertain.\n\nLow (10-20%) chance of isolated thunderstorm development near and \neast of US127 this afternoon as eroding MLCIN couples with an \napproaching shortwave. However, a lack of any kind of mesoscale \nforcing/surface boundary should limit storm development. Ample DCAPE \nwith 30-45 knots of deep layer shear and around 1000 J/kg MLCAPE \ncould support storm organization with damaging winds as the main \nhazard and large hail also possible.\n\nAttention then turns to a mid-level wave across the Northern Plains \nas seen in satellite imagery. This wave is forecast to initiate \nstorms later today across northern Wisconsin and Minnesota. These \nstorms are forecast to propagate southeast along the instability \ngradient. Two scenarios remain possible as of this package. First is \nthat the storms take a more southerly track remaining over Lake \nMichigan. Second is the instability makes it a bit further east \nallowing one or more clusters with embedded bowing structures to \nimpact the CWA, best chance near and west of US-131. Short range \nguidance is split on which scenario occurs, with mesoscale analysis \ninto the evening being critical for identifying which scenario \noccurs. Large hail and damaging winds are the main threats. Greatest \nrisk window is 10pm tonight through 6 am Monday.\n\nBottom line is strong to severe storms are possible today and \ntonight, greatest risk west of US-131. However, depending on storm \ntrack, many areas could stay dry through tonight or see just garden \nvariety showers and storms.\n\n- Severe thunderstorms possible Monday afternoon/evening.\n  Damaging winds are the main risk with all hazards possible. Timing \n  and coverage remain uncertain.\n\nThe aforementioned convective uncertainty carries over into the \nforecast for Monday afternoon and evening as debris clouds and or \nboundaries from earlier storms will affect the degree of \ndestabilization by evening as well as potential locations for storm \ninitiation.\n\nShort range model guidance does paint a potent environment for \nMonday evening as lower Michigan sets up in the left exit region of \na mid-level jet. This will lead to over 50 knots of deep layer shear \nacross West Michigan. Storms are forecast to initiate off a surface \ntrough/cold front Monday evening and track southeast with time. If \nthe forecast destabilization occurs, a very favorable environment \nwill be in place for thunderstorms to be severe. Strong kinematics \nand north of 1200 J/kg DCAPE supports a damaging wind threat with \nconcern for winds in excess of 70 mph in any bowing structures that \nmay materialize. Initial development given the kinematics at play \nlikely is supercellular if a favorable environment is realized. \nStrong shear could support large hail in discrete storms as well. In \naddition, can't rule out a tornado or two southwest of a Muskegon to \nJackson line. However, uncertainty persists in the strength of the \nLLJ and associated low-level shear available Monday evening.\n\nOverall, we have a conditionally favorable environment for severe \nweather (possibly significant) Monday evening. If we have a favorable \nthermodynamic environment, severe weather is possible areawide, \nespecially the southern 2/3 of the forecast area. However, this \nsetup will come down to mesoscale details with a chance that \novernight storms limit intensity/development of storms Monday \nevening. With a range of potential solutions still present across \nguidance, the forecast remains uncertain and changes are very \npossible into Monday.\n\n- Cooler With Chance of Showers Friday\n\nPost cold-frontal conditions are likely to see slightly cooler \nconditons as well as dewpoints falling back into the 60s. Troughing \naloft, a dry low-level airmass, and surface ridging are likely to \nkeep conditons dry until mid-week. A frontal boundary and attendant \nshortwave Friday then bring a chance of showers back to the area to \nfinish the work week.",
